## 1. Overview

AWS Transit Gateway is a service that simplifies connecting Amazon VPCs and on-premises networks. It acts as a hub that controls how traffic is routed among all the connected networks, eliminating the need for complex peering relationships.

### Key Characteristics

- **Centralized Hub** - Single point of connectivity for all networks
- **Simplifies Architecture** - Eliminates VPC-to-VPC peering
- **Multi-Region Support** - Connect gateways across regions
- **On-Premises Integration** - Direct Connect and VPN attachments
- **Network Segmentation** - Route tables for isolation
- **Scalability** - Handles thousands of VPC attachments
- **High Availability** - Built-in redundancy and failover
- **Monitoring** - CloudWatch metrics and Flow Logs

### Transit Gateway vs VPC Peering

| Feature | Transit Gateway | VPC Peering |
|---------|---|---|
| Connections | Hub-and-spoke | Mesh (each to each) |
| Scalability | 1000+ attachments | Hundreds of peerings |
| Management | Centralized | Decentralized |
| Setup Complexity | Simple | Complex at scale |
| Multi-region | Native support | Manual setup |
| On-premises | Direct integration | Via VPN |
| Monitoring | Centralized | Per-peering |
| Cost | Per attachment/hour | Per peering connection |
| Network Segmentation | Via route tables | None (flat network) |
| Transit Routes | Supported | Not supported |
| DNS Resolution | Enhanced | Basic |

