## 7. Network Segmentation

### Isolation Strategy

```
Example: Compliance Isolation

┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│      Transit Gateway                │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                     │
│  Route Table: Production            │
│  ├─ VPC-1 (Prod)                   │
│  ├─ VPC-2 (Prod)                   │
│  ├─ Route: 10.0.0.0/8 → On-Prem   │
│  └─ Route: 10.100.0.0/16 → DX     │
│                                     │
│  Route Table: Development           │
│  ├─ VPC-3 (Dev)                    │
│  ├─ VPC-4 (Dev)                    │
│  └─ Route: 10.10.0.0/8 → On-Prem  │
│     (No access to Prod or On-Prem) │
│                                     │
│  Route Table: Shared                │
│  ├─ VPC-5 (Logging)                │
│  ├─ VPC-6 (DNS)                    │
│  └─ No restrictions (accessible)   │
│                                     │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Implementation

```bash
# Production route table (only prod networks)
PROD_RT=$(aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-id $TGW \
  --query 'TransitGatewayRouteTable.TransitGatewayRouteTableId' \
  --output text)

# Development route table (isolated)
DEV_RT=$(aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-id $TGW \
  --query 'TransitGatewayRouteTable.TransitGatewayRouteTableId' \
  --output text)

# Shared route table (all can access)
SHARED_RT=$(aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-id $TGW \
  --query 'TransitGatewayRouteTable.TransitGatewayRouteTableId' \
  --output text)

# Associate production VPCs to production route table
aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $PROD_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_PROD_1

aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $PROD_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_PROD_2

# Associate dev VPCs to dev route table
aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $DEV_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_DEV_1

aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $DEV_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_DEV_2

# Production route table: Route to on-premises
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $PROD_RT \
  --destination-cidr-block 10.0.0.0/8 \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $DX_ATTACH

# Development route table: No on-premises access (blackhole or no route)
# Result: Dev VPCs cannot reach on-premises networks
```

### Shared Services Access

```bash
# Common pattern: Shared services accessible to all

# Associate shared VPCs to shared route table
aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $SHARED_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_LOGGING

aws ec2 associate-transit-gateway-route-table \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $SHARED_RT \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_DNS

# Add routes from production to shared services
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $PROD_RT \
  --destination-cidr-block 10.50.0.0/16 \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_LOGGING

# Add routes from dev to shared services
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-route \
  --transit-gateway-route-table-id $DEV_RT \
  --destination-cidr-block 10.50.0.0/16 \
  --transit-gateway-attachment-id $VPC_LOGGING

# Result: Prod and Dev can access logging VPC, but not each other
```

